# Launcher update signing key

Self-update verifies `watt-start-update.json` with minisign when
`packaging/update-key.pub` exists at compile time. This repository does not
ship a key yet. Create one on a machine you control, and never commit the
secret half.

```sh
minisign -G -p packaging/update-key.pub -s watt-start-update.key
```

minisign asks for a password. Keep it; the release workflow needs it.
`-s` defaults to `~/.minisign/minisign.key` if you omit it. Put the secret
somewhere outside the repository either way. `watt-start-update.key`,
`minisign.key` and `*.sec` are gitignored.

Then, in the GitHub repository settings, add two Actions secrets:

- `MINISIGN_SECRET_KEY`: the whole secret-key file, comment lines included.
- `MINISIGN_PASSWORD`: its password. minisign reads it from stdin in CI.

A key made with `minisign -G -W` has no password; leave `MINISIGN_PASSWORD`
unset for it. Keep an offline backup of the secret key: losing it means the
next release cannot be verified by builds that trust this key (see rotation in
`docs/releasing.md`).

Commit `packaging/update-key.pub` when builds should require a signature.
Until that file exists, release binaries treat the manifest as unsigned
(HTTPS plus the sha256 in the manifest) and the About card says so. Once it is
committed, every release must be signed, so set the secrets first.

Rotation, what the workflow does when the secret is absent, and manual
rollback are in `docs/releasing.md`.
